{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"EVIDENCE for the acceptance criterion \"Cancellation rate is either explained as expected or reduced\" — it is EXPECTED, and no work is lost.\n\n## Measurement\n\nSampled 15 cancelled `Reconcile Bridge` runs via the Actions jobs API:\n\n    sampled: 15 | cancelled-while-pending (no work): 15 | executed steps: 0 | api errors: 0\n\nEvery one had **no jobs ever created** — zero executed steps. Not a single cancelled run\nhad started the reconciler, touched the pass lock, mutated Jira, or written a ticket\nevent. A separate spot-check of 3 more recent cancellations (30592231406, 30590245682,\n30588021254) gave the same result.\n\n## Mechanism\n\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` declares:\n\n    concurrency:\n      group: reconcile-bridge\n      cancel-in-progress: false\n\n`cancel-in-progress: false` means a RUNNING pass is never interrupted — it always\ncompletes. GitHub, however, keeps at most ONE run *pending* per concurrency group: when\na newer run is queued behind an already-pending one, the older pending run is cancelled\nas superseded.\n\nThe bridge feeds that queue from two sources at once — the `*/20` cron backstop and the\n`RECONCILE_CONTINUOUS` re-dispatch (each run dispatching its successor). With a pass\ntaking minutes, arrivals routinely outpace the single-flight group, so superseded\n*pending* runs are the steady state.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe ~15% cancellation rate is queue collapse, not aborted work. It is the designed\nbehaviour of `cancel-in-progress: false` under a deliberately over-fed schedule, and it\nis exactly what protects a running pass from being killed mid-flight. Reducing it would\nmean either slowing the continuous loop or dropping the cron backstop — both of which\ntrade freshness for a cosmetically cleaner run list.\n\nI originally listed the cancellation volume in this ticket's description as a possible\ncommon driver behind modes 1 and 2. That inference is now RETRACTED: a run that never\nstarts cannot contend for the pass lock or the tickets branch, so cancellations cannot\nbe causing either failure mode. The real driver of contention is the continuous\nre-dispatch producing overlapping *successful* runs, which is a separate matter and is\ncovered by 4c4f-e554-849a-4dfd.\n\nCriterion satisfied as \"explained as expected\". No change proposed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785460751144515001,"uuid":"d1e265a2-bbe9-4b6d-bdea-5a8ff41aebae"}